// ============================================================
// CHARACTER: Andrew — Disciple
// ACT: Act II
// CASE: The End of the Age
// CASE ID: olivet_discourse
// SOURCE: NPC 'andrew_olivet'
// BIBLE REFERENCE: Mark 13:3–8; Matthew 24:3–14
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PURPOSE: Andrew describes the Olivet Discourse — signs and commission to endure
// ============================================================

-> start
=== start ===
He spoke of the end, Scribe. Not just of our lives, but of those great stones down there. He says not one will be left standing on another.
* [When will these things happen?] -> signs
* [The Temple is eternal, surely?] -> doom


=== signs ===
He warned us of deceptions and wars — men claiming to be the Messiah, nations rising against nations, famines and earthquakes. But He called those birth pains, not the end itself. Then He said the Good News must reach all nations before the end comes. It is a heavy burden and a clear commission.
* [A long road before the end.] -> long_road
* [What should we watch for?] -> watch_signs


=== doom ===
That is what we thought. But look at His face — He was not guessing. He was mourning what He knows is coming. He has looked at this city and loved it and He is describing its destruction without flinching. A man who weeps over Jerusalem does not exaggerate its fate.
* [He wept over Jerusalem.] -> weeping_city
* [Then the Temple will fall.] -> temple_fall

=== long_road ===
Peter and I asked about the timing — when and what sign. He told us to be careful not to be deceived. Wars and disasters would happen, but they were not the end. He was telling us not to panic and not to give up. Stay at the task. Endure to the end. That is the command.
* [And the Good News to all nations?] -> nations_commission
* [He was telling you what to expect, not what to fear.] -> watch_signs

=== watch_signs ===
He spoke of a specific sign — the desolating sacrilege standing in the holy place, the signal to flee. He warned of false christs performing signs, even able to deceive the elect if possible. The instruction was to test, not to follow every voice. Endure, watch, and pray.
* [A future specific enough to act on.] -> nations_commission
* [Pray because we cannot know the hour.] -> end_words

=== weeping_city ===
Yes. On the road into the city a few days before this. He wept aloud — "If you had known on this day what would bring you peace. But now it is hidden from your eyes." He knew exactly what the rejection meant for Jerusalem. The Olivet Discourse is the explanation of that weeping.
* [Judgment spoken with grief.] -> nations_commission

=== temple_fall ===
Within a generation, He said. Those stones — the ones we were admiring as we left, each one the size of a house — not one will be left. The nation that rejects its king will lose the institution that defined it. It is the pattern of every prior judgment in the Scripture.
* [And the people?] -> nations_commission

=== nations_commission ===
This is the part I carry with me most. He turned from the destruction of the Temple and the cosmic signs — and He spoke about mission. Before the end, the gospel of the kingdom will be proclaimed to all nations. The Temple falls. The mission continues. The two are not contradictions. The end of one system is the beginning of a wider one.
* [The Jewish centre disperses to the whole world.] -> end_words
* [And then the end comes.] -> end_words

=== end_words ===
Watch and pray. Those were his final words on the subject. Not dates, not calculations. Watch and pray. He left us with the responsibility to stay awake without knowing the timetable. I think that was deliberate.
* [Faith without certainty about timing.] -> closing

=== closing ===
He trusts us with enough to act, but not enough to stop watching. I used to think I wanted the date. Now I think knowing the date would ruin the waiting. The watching is the point.
-> DONE